('127.256.d.11.A',3,'Accounting is regulated so that financial reports are:','["Costly for no reason", "Random", "Secret", "Reliable and trustworthy"]'::jsonb,3,'Regulation protects users.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.A',4,'Rules that require public companies to have their financial statements audited are intended to protect:','["Investors", "Rivals", "Competitors", "Landlords"]'::jsonb,0,'Audits assure accuracy.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.B',3,'The stock market crash of 1929 and the Great Depression led Congress to create the:','["Peace Corps", "Securities and Exchange Commission", "Interstate system", "Postal Service"]'::jsonb,1,'The SEC was created in 1934.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.B',4,'Accounting scandals in the early 2000s, such as Enron, led to the:','["Homestead Act", "Clayton Act", "Sarbanes-Oxley Act", "GI Bill"]'::jsonb,2,'SOX followed the scandals.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.C',3,'The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 requires top executives to:','["Pay no taxes", "Hide losses", "Avoid audits", "Certify the accuracy of financial reports"]'::jsonb,3,'Executives are personally accountable.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.C',4,'Section 404 of Sarbanes-Oxley focuses on:','["Internal controls over financial reporting", "Marketing", "Hiring", "Pricing"]'::jsonb,0,'Controls must be assessed.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.D',3,'The SEC requires public companies to file regular reports such as the:','["1099", "10-K annual report", "1040", "W-2"]'::jsonb,1,'Form 10-K is annual.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.D',4,'The SEC''s role includes enforcing securities laws and:','["Setting store prices", "Printing money", "Protecting investors", "Building roads"]'::jsonb,2,'Investor protection.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.E',3,'In Texas, CPAs are licensed and regulated by the:','["Texas Rangers", "Texas Lottery", "Texas Highway Department", "Texas State Board of Public Accountancy"]'::jsonb,3,'The state board licenses CPAs.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.E',4,'State boards of accountancy can discipline CPAs who:','["Violate professional standards", "Take vacations", "Move", "Retire"]'::jsonb,0,'They enforce standards.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.F.i',3,'The first step of researching a regulatory case study is to:','["Skip sources", "Form questions to guide the research", "Copy a summary", "Write the conclusion"]'::jsonb,1,'Questions focus the work.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.F.i',4,'A strong research question about the Great Depression and bank reform might ask:','["What is a bank?", "Was it long ago?", "How did bank failures lead to deposit insurance?", "Is money nice?"]'::jsonb,2,'Good questions are specific and analytical.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.F.ii',3,'Reliable sources for researching a regulatory case include:','["Rumors", "Jokes", "Anonymous posts", "Government reports, court records, and respected news outlets"]'::jsonb,3,'Use credible sources.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.F.ii',4,'A primary source for studying the Securities Act of 1933 is:','["The text of the law itself", "A fan post", "A cartoon", "A friend''s memory"]'::jsonb,0,'Original text is primary.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.F.iii',3,'To judge whether a source is reliable, a researcher should check the:','["Font", "Author''s credentials and the evidence cited", "Length", "Color"]'::jsonb,1,'Evaluate authority and evidence.','original'),
('127.256.d.11.F.iii',4,'Two sources disagree about the date of a law. The researcher should:','["Average them", "Pick one", "Check the official text or a government source", "Ignore both"]'::jsonb,2,'Verify with authoritative sources.','original'),
